{
  "title": "La proposta {ip:va} rivista: si {ip:ritiene} che {csp:sia} troppo costosa",
  "translation": "The proposal must be revised: it is considered too expensive",
  "topics": "Passive constructions with essere, venire, andare and rimanere, impersonal and passive si",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Choosing the right passive"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "What it adds",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "essere",
              "neutral, all tenses",
              "La proposta {pp:è stata} {pp:rivista}."
            ],
            [
              "venire",
              "the process, simple tenses only",
              "La proposta {ip:viene} discussa ogni anno."
            ],
            [
              "andare",
              "obligation, simple tenses only",
              "La proposta {ip:va} rivista."
            ],
            [
              "rimanere",
              "an unexpected result",
              "{pp:È rimasto} ferito nell'incidente."
            ],
            [
              "essere da + infinitive",
              "something still to be done",
              "La proposta {ip:è} da rivedere."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*Andare* with verbs such as *perdere, smarrire* or *distruggere* does not express an obligation but a loss: *I documenti {pp:sono andati} perduti.*",
      {
        "h": "The passive in formal and academic writing"
      },
      "Omitting the agent makes a text sound impersonal and objective. That is why it is common in reports and science: *I dati {pp:sono stati} {pp:raccolti} in tre fasi*. Use it deliberately, because a long chain of passives quickly makes a text heavy.",
      {
        "h": "Si with verbs of thinking and saying"
      },
      "These impersonal expressions present an opinion as generally held. After a verb of opinion, formal Italian uses the *congiuntivo*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Expression",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "si {ip:ritiene} che",
              "Si {ip:ritiene} che {csp:sia} troppo costosa.",
              "It is considered…"
            ],
            [
              "si {ip:pensa} che",
              "Si {ip:pensa} che {csi:fosse} un pittore.",
              "He is thought to have been…"
            ],
            [
              "si {ip:dice} che",
              "Si {ip:dice} che {csa:abbia venduto} tutto.",
              "They say that…"
            ],
            [
              "si {ip:prevede} che",
              "Si {ip:prevede} che aumenterà.",
              "It is expected that…"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*Si {ip:prevede}* looks to the future, so it usually takes the future: *Si {ip:prevede} che i prezzi aumenteranno*.",
      {
        "h": "Agreement with si: a full summary"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Case",
            "Verb",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "No object",
              "singular",
              "Si {pp:è parlato} molto."
            ],
            [
              "Singular object",
              "singular, agrees",
              "Si {pp:è venduta} la casa."
            ],
            [
              "Plural object",
              "plural, agrees",
              "Si {pp:sono vendut**i**} tutti i biglietti."
            ],
            [
              "Verb normally with essere",
              "participle plural",
              "Si {pp:è partit**i**} presto."
            ],
            [
              "Adjective after essere",
              "adjective plural",
              "Si {ip:è} content**i**."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Unlike the passive with *essere*, a sentence with *si* can't name the agent: *Si {pp:è venduta} la casa da Marco* is wrong."
    ]
  },
